/**
\fn Part * pdg::Sprite::transferPart(Part * part, bool includeDescendants)
\brief Transfer an existing Part and optional subtree into this Sprite.

Moves the same Part objects; retained Part, PhysicsBody, Collider and internal
PhysicsConstraint handles remain valid. Names and Part-owned Image/Drawing content
are retained. The destination assigns fresh per-Sprite IDs and appends the moved
Parts in their source order. Read getId() again after transfer. Transferring a Part
already owned by this Sprite is a no-op.

World transforms, body mass/inertia, velocities, loads, manual world-space drives
and collision settings are preserved. The moved root loses its old Part parent;
parents inside the transferred set are retained. With includeDescendants false,
children left behind keep their world placement and nearest remaining Part ancestor.
Movement, rotation and scale programming is canceled on Parts whose parent frame
changes. Logical size animation and other local state remain.

Descendants include ordinary Part children, physical-rig bone descendants and
registered assembly children. Joints within the selected set survive. Joints to
any body outside it disconnect; retained handles report inactive. Source assembly
mass decreases by the transferred member masses without redistributing the others.
The destination does not automatically register the transferred bodies in its rig:
use attachAnimationPhysicsPart() and create any new joints explicitly.

Mapped rig bodies are released from the source controller and become ordinary
Dynamic Part bodies. At least one attached skeletal body must remain in the source
rig; transferring its selected root chooses a remaining root using the usual rule.
Source bone/artwork/socket bindings are cleared rather than rebound by name. The
source skeleton, animation clips, imported artwork and animation-drawing registrations
remain source-owned; use Part.setDrawing()/setImage() for artwork that moves with
the Part. The source rig can subsequently be disabled or destroyed without removing
the transferred bodies. Layer snapshots preserve the resulting ownership and joints.

\param part Part currently owned by a Sprite.
\param includeDescendants Transfer descendants too; optional, default true.
\return The same Part, now owned by this Sprite. The pointer is borrowed; use
addRef()/release() if retaining it beyond removal or destination destruction.

\note Both Sprites must share a layer, or both be off-layer. Names must be unique
in the destination. Singular/sheared placements that cannot be represented by Part
transforms and Sprite-mount cycles reject before transfer. Call outside animation
modifiers, drawing callbacks and locked physics callbacks. Finish or cancel source
rig recovery first. Clear any Part IK target involving a selected Part; re-establish
it after transfer. Release manual drives before detaching attached skeletal bodies,
following the existing explicit drive-handoff contract. Independent or already
detached bodies retain their manual drives. Parent-relative IK limits are cleared
when their frame changes. The example assumes a physical weight Part and a hand
in the receiving Sprite's active rig.

\par Example
\code{.cpp}
auto* weight = receiver.transferPart(donor.findPart("weight"));
auto* hand = receiver.findPart("hand");
receiver.attachAnimationPhysicsPart(weight, hand);
hand->physics.createPivotJoint(weight->physics);
\endcode

\see createPart(), Part::getId(), attachAnimationPhysicsPart(), detachAnimationPhysicsPart()
*/
